--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Wednesday, the 19th of March, 1930 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
I spoke to Mr. Walter Thomas, the manager, at half past seven this morning, and was set to work to clean the Moorgate Street offices of the Moorgate Insurance Company. At nine o'clock I had finished, and went on to the Finsbury Circus offices of the same concern. At a quarter to ten I had done there, and went down to the washhouse in the basement, and washed up the dirty towels. Then I came back to the sitting-room, and did a little plain sewing till noon. At noon I had dinner, and, after dinner, I cleared away, and spent the afternoon sewing till five o'clock. At five I went out and walked home to Camberwell, and walked back again at six. Washing up and supper followed, and at half past six I began to read the evening paper aloud to Bert. At eight o'clock I shut up, and went to bed.
   ◇ Arthur reflects: He thinks Joan Hale is a truthful little girl, who has a bad cold, but will mend.
   ◇ Arthur reflects: He thinks that Mrs. Edith Hale fears that Joan is ill.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Wednesday, the 19th of March, 1930 (1/3 key facts kept) ---
